--- !u!114 &677528541
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 677528537}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 031d64bd0152bfa43a6f53fd64858c29, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::BrieferCode
  animatorBrief: {fileID: 677528540}
  cameraTarget: {fileID: 0}
  overallWeight: 1
  bodyWeight: 0.2
  headWeight: 0.8
  eyesWeight: 1
  clampWeight: 0.5
  dialogue:
  - "Hello there. Let me tell you about something important happening here in Namibia.
    Some elephants you may see out in the wild are fitted with GPS tracking collars.
    These are called collared elephants. The collars allow conservation teams to
    monitor their movements in real time. This technology helps us understand where
    elephants travel, when they approach farms or villages, and how we can respond
    before problems arise. It\u2019s one of the most important tools we have to help
    both people and elephants live safely alongside each other. Do you have any questions
    so far?"
  - Now, you might be wondering why we collar elephants in the first place. There
    are several important reasons. First, it provides early warning. When elephants
    move toward villages, farms, or shared water sources, communities can be alerted
    in advance and prepare. Second, it helps us understand elephant movement. Elephants
    travel across large areas in search of food and water, and tracking shows migration
    routes, seasonal patterns, and where they come into contact with people. Third,
    it reduces human-elephant conflict. When elephant movements are known, communities
    and wildlife officers can prepare in advance and avoid dangerous surprises, crop
    damage, and property destruction. Finally, it supports conservation planning.
    The data helps experts design wildlife corridors and land-use strategies that
    allow elephants to move safely while reducing risks to people. Any questions
    about that?
  - Living near elephants is not always easy. Roaming elephants can create serious
    challenges for communities. They may destroy farmlands by eating or trampling
    crops, which can be devastating for families who depend on farming for food and
    income. They can also damage fences and infrastructure, as they are powerful
    animals capable of breaking barriers and water installations. There is also a
    risk to human safety. Unexpected encounters between people and elephants can
    be dangerous and may lead to injuries or even loss of life. When damage happens
    repeatedly, communities may become frustrated and retaliate against elephants.
    This puts elephant populations at risk and makes conservation more difficult.
    This situation is known as human-elephant conflict, where people and elephants
    compete for space and resources. Does that make sense, or would you like me to
    explain anything further?
  - "Now let\u2019s look at how the collaring process works. First, the elephant
    is located, either from the air or on the ground. A veterinarian then darts the
    elephant with a tranquilizer. After being darted, the elephant may move for a
    short distance before becoming calm enough for the team to approach. Once the
    team reaches the elephant, their first priority is to make sure it can breathe
    properly. The trunk must be kept clear, and sometimes a branch is placed inside
    to help keep the airway open. Next, the team fits the tracking collar. If the
    elephant already has an old collar, it is removed first. The new collar is then
    placed around the neck and secured. Because of the elephant\u2019s size, several
    people and sometimes ropes are needed to position the collar correctly. At the
    same time, the team may carry out other important tasks. These include clearing
    nearby bushes for safe access, measuring the elephant\u2019s body and tusks,
    pouring water over the elephant to prevent overheating, treating any visible
    injuries, checking vital signs, and taking blood samples if needed. Once everything
    is complete, the veterinarian administers a reversal drug. The team then moves
    away to give the elephant space to wake up and recover safely. Do you want me
    to go over any part of that again, and anythjing I need to show? "
  - "Collaring elephants also comes with challenges and risks. One major challenge
    is when the elephant does not fall onto its side. Because an elephant can weigh
    several tons, it is very difficult to reposition it. In such cases, additional
    people and sometimes even a vehicle are needed to move the elephant into a safer
    position. There are also important safety concerns. The area where the dart enters
    the elephant\u2019s body is highly toxic to humans. For this reason, the site
    may be marked with blue spray paint to show that it must not be touched. These
    precautions are essential to keep both the team and the animal safe. Any questions
    before we move on?"
  - "In the end, elephant collaring is about more than just tracking animals. It
    is about creating a balance between people and wildlife. By understanding elephant
    movements, we can reduce conflict, protect communities, and ensure that elephants
    continue to move safely through their natural habitats. Coexistence is possible,
    but it requires knowledge, planning, and cooperation. Anything you\u2019d like
    to ask before we finish?"
